1
00:00:00,020 --> 00:00:00,860
Hi guys.

2
00:00:00,860 --> 00:00:07,580
Let's talk about Agile, Scrum, Kanban and daily meetings.

3
00:00:07,580 --> 00:00:11,360
Why do people have to run those meetings?

4
00:00:12,860 --> 00:00:17,570
Agile is the methodology of software development.

5
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:22,970
Its ability to be flexible and respond to change.

6
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:27,560
What changes might we have during the software development?

7
00:00:27,770 --> 00:00:33,800
Changes can be new requirements from the client, new timelines and priorities.

8
00:00:35,980 --> 00:00:43,570
Agile is divided into two main types methodologies Scrum and Kanban.

9
00:00:43,960 --> 00:00:49,600
Let's have a look what these two types have in common and what is different.

10
00:00:50,290 --> 00:00:55,840
So both Scrum and Kanban are agile, flexible.

11
00:00:55,840 --> 00:00:58,510
They respond to change quickly.

12
00:00:58,660 --> 00:01:01,060
Both use pull techniques.

13
00:01:01,060 --> 00:01:06,250
When you pull the task from the list of tasks to do.

14
00:01:06,610 --> 00:01:10,210
Both use boards to visualize the progress.

15
00:01:10,210 --> 00:01:14,410
So on the picture you can see example of a Kanban board.

16
00:01:14,770 --> 00:01:21,550
Both have organized teams and both focus on transparency.

17
00:01:21,550 --> 00:01:29,740
When everyone in the team knows what is happening and what the progress on the project is.

18
00:01:30,900 --> 00:01:33,690
What is different in Scrum?

19
00:01:33,690 --> 00:01:36,990
The roles of the team are clearly defined.

20
00:01:37,080 --> 00:01:41,000
In scrum, tasks are divided into sprints.

21
00:01:41,010 --> 00:01:45,030
Usually Sprint lasts for 1 or 2 weeks.

22
00:01:45,390 --> 00:01:55,830
In Scrum team has daily meetings to discuss the progress and scrum is best for teams with more stable

23
00:01:55,830 --> 00:01:56,790
priorities.

24
00:01:57,390 --> 00:02:03,030
Kanban is better when you have a lot of changes during the workflow.

25
00:02:03,150 --> 00:02:14,070
Kanban is more flexible and you can add tasks during the process and daily meetings in Kanban are not

26
00:02:14,100 --> 00:02:15,150
necessary.

27
00:02:16,660 --> 00:02:18,640
Let's focus on daily meetings.

28
00:02:18,670 --> 00:02:25,480
They are also known as stand up meetings, daily scrum or status meetings.

29
00:02:25,510 --> 00:02:33,430
These meetings are held every day, usually at the same time and place and same participants, and they

30
00:02:33,430 --> 00:02:36,700
lost no more than 15 minutes.

31
00:02:37,640 --> 00:02:46,630
Three main questions that people have to answer during these meetings are What did you do yesterday?

32
00:02:46,660 --> 00:02:48,600
What will you do today?

33
00:02:48,610 --> 00:02:51,700
And are there any blockers?

34
00:02:53,560 --> 00:02:56,530
How to answer these questions.

35
00:02:56,530 --> 00:03:05,020
If you are a developer, you can say something like Yesterday I finished refactoring navigation and

36
00:03:05,020 --> 00:03:12,400
I started Typekit DC 6789 to give your update for yesterday.

37
00:03:12,400 --> 00:03:14,620
You use past simple.

38
00:03:15,390 --> 00:03:20,050
Today I will complete Typekit DC 6789.

39
00:03:20,070 --> 00:03:28,500
I will send Typekit for review and I am going to fix the issue with Google sign in for today.

40
00:03:28,500 --> 00:03:31,320
You can use future simple.

41
00:03:31,320 --> 00:03:32,520
I will complete.

42
00:03:32,520 --> 00:03:36,990
I will send or present continuous.

43
00:03:36,990 --> 00:03:38,610
I am going to fix.

44
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:43,650
If you don't have any blockers you say I don't have any blockers.

45
00:03:43,680 --> 00:03:43,980
Okay.

46
00:03:44,010 --> 00:03:47,430
Pause the video and repeat the update.

47
00:03:49,640 --> 00:03:53,090
What to say if you do have some blockers.

48
00:03:53,240 --> 00:04:00,980
You can say phrases like, I don't have experience using some technology, so I need more time to figure

49
00:04:00,980 --> 00:04:01,940
it out.

50
00:04:02,330 --> 00:04:05,900
I'm having trouble debugging something.

51
00:04:06,020 --> 00:04:10,340
I need an approval from someone to do something.

52
00:04:10,550 --> 00:04:18,800
I need help repairing some issue because it stopped working or I'm waiting for an access to, let's

53
00:04:18,800 --> 00:04:20,390
say, AWS.

54
00:04:20,510 --> 00:04:25,430
Okay, Pause the video and repeat the phrases from my screen.

55
00:04:26,500 --> 00:04:27,370
Good job.

56
00:04:28,060 --> 00:04:30,850
The goal of daily meetings.

57
00:04:30,850 --> 00:04:35,860
Why people need to have them to check status to give update.

58
00:04:35,890 --> 00:04:42,330
What is the progress on the project and to get everyone on the team on the same page?

59
00:04:42,340 --> 00:04:47,230
But daily meetings are not to solve problems.

60
00:04:47,530 --> 00:04:52,600
Let's have a look at this short dialogue.

61
00:04:52,960 --> 00:04:59,980
Tom I don't have experience using AWS, so I need more time to figure it out.

62
00:04:59,980 --> 00:05:03,480
So Tom is talking about his blocker.

63
00:05:03,820 --> 00:05:07,320
Sarah I can show you how to use it.

64
00:05:07,330 --> 00:05:09,280
You just need to.

65
00:05:09,280 --> 00:05:14,860
And Sarah wants to help Tom to explain how to solve his blocker.

66
00:05:15,250 --> 00:05:22,900
Bruce says, Why don't you guys just have a call after the meeting in order to solve this problem?

67
00:05:23,080 --> 00:05:32,720
Bruce knows that daily meetings are not to solve issues or deal with blockers, and he suggests guys

68
00:05:32,720 --> 00:05:36,440
to do that after the daily meeting.

69
00:05:36,440 --> 00:05:38,990
That's a good way to do it.

70
00:05:39,790 --> 00:05:41,740
Okay, time for practice.

71
00:05:41,770 --> 00:05:44,610
Go to your file and do the tasks.

72
00:05:44,620 --> 00:05:47,620
Thank you and see you on the next lesson.

73
00:05:47,620 --> 00:05:48,160
Bye.
